---
functor: 1
title: Diagrams and labelled arrows
---

set heading(numbering: none)

A diagram is a grid with arrows. A matrix is a grid with a fence. Both use the same cells: a comma ends a cell and a semicolon ends a row.

| Structure | Positions | Relations |
|:--|:--|:--|
| `mat`, `vec`, `cases`, `binom` | a grid | a fence |
| a chain in display math | rows | the align point |
| `diagram` | a grid | arrows between cells |

# Labelled arrows

A script on a relation is a label. `^` puts the label above, and `_` puts it below. The arrow grows with `wide` to fit the label.

| Write | Result |
|:--|:-:|
| `A to^f B` | $A to^f B$ |
| `A to_p B` | $A to_p B$ |
| `x =^"def" y` | $x =^"def" y$ |
| `0 to A to^f B to^g C to 0` | $0 to A to^f B to^g C to 0$ |

This works on any relation. A relation with a long straight part, such as `to`, `=`, `implies` or `mapsto`, grows. A relation with no straight part, such as `sim`, keeps its size and stays in the middle.

# diagram

Write the cells, then `|`, then the arrows. Write each arrow as a map: `f: A to B`.

```fn
$$
diagram(
  A, B;
  C, D
| f: A to B, g: A to C, h: B to D, k: C to D)
$$
```

It gives:

$$
diagram(A, B; C, D | f: A to B, g: A to C, h: B to D, k: C to D)
$$

- An arrow names its cells by their content. If two cells have the same content, write the position: `2.1` is row 2, column 1.
- The arrow is any relation: `to`, `mapsto`, `implies`, `=`, `sim`, or a `sym` that you build. On a diagonal or a vertical edge, the same glyph turns to the angle.
- `f: A to B` puts the label f on the outer side of the diagram. `A to^f B` puts it on the left of the arrow direction, and `A to_f B` on the right.
- A cell can be empty: `diagram(A, ; B, C | A to C)`.
- Do not write a bar `|` in a cell. Use `abs(x)`.

```fn
$$
diagram(X times Y, Y; X, Z | p_2: X times Y to Y, p_1: X times Y to X, g: Y to Z, f: X to Z, X times Y to Z)
$$
```

$$
diagram(X times Y, Y; X, Z | p_2: X times Y to Y, p_1: X times Y to X, g: Y to Z, f: X to Z, X times Y to Z)
$$

| Rule | Values | Default |
|:--|:--|:--|
| `set diagram(gap: ...)` | `small`, `normal`, `large` | `normal` |
| `set diagram(inline: ...)` | `small`, `full` | `small` |

The gap between two cells also grows to fit a long label.

# Inline structures

Inline is a context, so it is a style rule and not new syntax. In running text, `mat`, `vec` (with components in a column) and `diagram` are small by default, so the lines stay evenly spaced. `set mat(inline: full)` keeps them full size.

The matrix $mat(a, b; c, d)$ is small in text. A one-row sequence such as $0 to A to^f B to 0$ needs no diagram.

# Not in Functor 1

Curved arrows, arrows that cross other arrows, and arrows between arrows (2-cells) come in a later phase. A curved arrow needs a bend deform.
